{"title":"Rational approximation of famous numbers","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p><b>Rational approximation</b> is the application of <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Rational_approximation\" title=\"Rational approximation\">Dirichlet's theorem</a> which shows that, for each irrational number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/7/8f79058700fd8cb5981ea6852cc4d911c81fdfb5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x\\in\\mathbb R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"45\" height=\"13\" />, the inequality <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/d/9bd4df44fdfd5e5d1ddf609da4a957f5650a2e04.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left|x-\\frac pq\\right|&lt;\\frac 1{q^2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"100\" height=\"43\" /> has infinitely many solutions. On the other hand, sometimes it is useful to know that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> cannot be approximated by rationals too well, or, more precisely, that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> is not a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Liouvillian_number&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Liouvillian number (page does not exist)\">Liouvillian number</a>, i.e., that for some power <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/6/6/5662060c982b6075a5a8f8211c335f501e0dcdbd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M&lt;+\\infty$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"75\" height=\"14\" />, the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Inequality\" title=\"Inequality\">inequality</a> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/3/e/23ed4415a4aa42a198918b2bd4181868bde6d5c8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left|x-\\frac pq\\right|\\ge \\frac 1{q^M}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"108\" height=\"43\" /> holds for all sufficiently large denominators <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/1/0615acc3725de21025457e7d6f7694dab8e2f758.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />. \n</p>\n<div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Theorem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Theorem</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Proof\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Proof</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Magic_Polynomial\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Magic Polynomial</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Example\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Example</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#A_Useful_Integral\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">A Useful Integral</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Polynomial\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3.2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Polynomial</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Estimates_of_the_Integral\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3.3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Estimates of the Integral</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Estimate_of\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3.4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Estimate of</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#Estimate_of_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3.5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Estimate of</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-10\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Theorem\">Theorem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Rational_approximation_of_famous_numbers&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Theorem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Suppose that there exist <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/d/c/0dcfa49fe43ce5b96a7286fb5b2456aa25a44e31.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0&lt;\\beta&lt;\\gamma&lt;1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"111\" height=\"16\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/e/2/ee25cb7272b6447bdccc297fa242a1a0c39156be.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1&lt;Q&lt;+\\infty$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"103\" height=\"16\" />\nand a sequence of pairs of integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/2/8/728ba3c0d6a6dc0a3056f4d63b1e7828ada057fb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(P_n,Q_n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"65\" height=\"18\" /> such that for all sufficiently large <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/b/3/db33a3e0dda5a8f03c17e4357dea22fbb1ad7b15.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|Q_n|\\le Q^n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"80\" height=\"18\" /> and \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/9/9/d99e8d33792df628b410c727e3ad6ad1eb623b85.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\beta^n&lt; \\left|P_n-Q_n x\\right|&lt;\\gamma^n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"174\" height=\"18\" />. Then, for every <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/1/9e12a096f79bab03d6e809a801fb6b5d9041f9b9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M&gt;\\frac{\\log(Q/\\beta)}{\\log(1/\\gamma)}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"118\" height=\"43\" />, the inequality <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/5/3454a193330291a0cf903ab4a19b18f42ee4ced6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left|x-\\frac pq\\right|&lt;\\frac 1{q^M}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"108\" height=\"43\" /> has only finitely many solutions.\n</p>\n<hr />\n<p>The exact formulation of the main theorem in this article is fitted to the Beukers proof of the non-Liouvillian character of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/c/f2ca003a7da0de4994b4733e203b74ff52d42553.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\pi$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" />, but the general spirit of all such theorems is the same: roughly speaking, they tell you that in order to show that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> cannot be approximated by rationals too well, one needs to find plenty of small, but not too small, linear combinations of  <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> with not too large integer coefficients.\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Proof\">Proof</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Rational_approximation_of_famous_numbers&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Proof\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Choose the least <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/7/cc7ac83c14af7b9422c224c835df2f74e30c6733.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gamma^n\\le 2q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"61\" height=\"16\" />. Note that for such choice of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/9/4/e94769cb7e9c607f902afdd54d5e122b507e6e69.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gamma^n&gt; \\frac {\\gamma}{2q}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -16px\" width=\"63\" height=\"36\" />. Also note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/1/6/616819886debc20439f8020af9339f06adcd9449.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Q_n\\ne 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"56\" height=\"17\" /> (otherwise <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/6/1/461e3af8f35f0591beac117a2c3035f78f31ab75.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|P_n|$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"29\" height=\"18\" /> would be an integer strictly between <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />). Now, there are two possible cases:\n</p><p><b>Case 1:</b> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/b/b5baed7c64fe99b60f1d89b22aa621f1061d2dc4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_n-Q_n\\frac pq=0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -16px\" width=\"112\" height=\"36\" />. \nThen <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/e/fae25c10d81b1566ac369a70da3afde37d79f72b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left|x-\\frac pq\\right|=\\left|x-\\frac {P_n}{Q_n}\\right|&gt;\\frac{\\beta^n}{|Q_n|}&gt;(\\beta/Q)^n =(\\gamma^n)^{\\frac{\\log(Q/\\beta)}{\\log(1/\\gamma)}}&gt; \\left(\\frac\\gamma{2q}\\right)^{\\frac{\\log(Q/\\beta)}{\\log(1/\\gamma)}}&gt;\\frac 1{q^M}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"579\" height=\"51\" /> \n</p><p>if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/1/0615acc3725de21025457e7d6f7694dab8e2f758.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> is large enough.\n</p><p><b>Case 2:</b> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/e/98e4f5b1e991be8bacf5501049e19661c4ea8928.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_n-Q_n\\frac pq\\ne 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -16px\" width=\"112\" height=\"36\" />. Then \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/8/f/78f0b5bd5fdeabab405bfb25a7f248a38cb3c0eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac 1q\\le\\left|P_n-Q_n\\frac pq\\right|\\le \\left|P_n-Q_n x\\right|+|Q_n|\\cdot\\left|x-\\frac pq\\right|\\le \\frac 1{2q}+Q^n\\left|x-\\frac pq\\right|$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"516\" height=\"43\" />\n</p><p>Hence, in this case,\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/b/c3b21a983fa52fc13f3b27cec9e5172d5a22a61b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left|x-\\frac pq\\right|\\ge \\frac 1{2q}Q^{-n}\\ge \\frac \\gamma{2q}Q^{-n}=\\frac \\gamma{2q}(\\gamma^n)^{\\frac{\\log Q}{\\log(1/\\gamma)}}\\ge \\left(\\frac\\gamma{2q}\\right)^{1+\\frac{\\log(Q}{\\log(1/\\gamma)}}&gt;\\frac 1{q^M}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"530\" height=\"51\" />\n</p><p>if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/1/0615acc3725de21025457e7d6f7694dab8e2f758.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> is large enough. (recall that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/7/9072c4a59d615c63928787d883ebae4b472f3ef0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\beta&lt;\\gamma$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"45\" height=\"16\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/b/a9bed8aa8c58525e45f2d338f42dc3cfd87dadb9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1+\\frac{\\log Q}{\\log(1/\\gamma)} =\\frac{\\log(Q/\\gamma)}{\\log(1/\\gamma)}&lt;\\frac{\\log(Q/\\beta)}{\\log(1/\\gamma)}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"304\" height=\"45\" />).\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Magic_Polynomial\">Magic Polynomial</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Rational_approximation_of_famous_numbers&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Magic Polynomial\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Before proceeding to the applications of the main theorem, let us introduce one very useful polynomial that often appears in proofs of irrationality. It is the polynomial \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/3/8/9382409721f47138d9986c7d8e270034555e43f6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(x)\\frac 1{n!}\\left(\\frac d{dx}\\right)^n [x^n(1-x)^n]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"216\" height=\"44\" />\n</p><p>Its coefficients can be easily computed using the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Binomial_Theorem\" title=\"Binomial Theorem\">binomial theorem</a>:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/b/1/0b1d649c6333ccf9354b112e6d7e907a9a01f8a2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(x)=\\sum_{k=0}^n (-1)^k{n+k\\choose n}{n\\choose k}x^k.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"266\" height=\"53\" />\n</p><p>The important points are that all the coefficients are integer and\nthe sum of their absolute values does not exceed\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/b/55b19d65c260aaf31794b974adb8cae08966b876.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\max_{0\\le k\\le n}{n+k\\choose n}\\sum_{0\\le k\\le n}{n\\choose  k}\\le {2n\\choose n}2^n\\le 8^n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"337\" height=\"54\" />.\n</p><p>Another useful remark is that the first <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/d/4bdca93962a2beaffc172ca394e04fe05bd74ffd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" /> derivatives of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/b/1/7b1baa027eff48cea7ea4613bb06469e24e81717.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^n(1-x)^n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"83\" height=\"18\" /> vanish at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, which makes the integration by parts extremely convenient:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/9/e/69ebd2aa3283d736dd75a8b302aaa6578e0dc7aa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\int_0^1 F(x)P(x)\\,dx=(-1)^n\\frac 1{n!}\\int_0^1 F^{(n)}(x) x^n(1-x)^n\\,dx.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"423\" height=\"46\" /> \n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Example\">Example</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Rational_approximation_of_famous_numbers&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Example\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>This section will prove that the number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/3/023f0d3201106a8fb6bdfe2f539eb1726736a453.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\ln 2$\" width=\"26\" height=\"12\" /> is not <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Liouvillian_number&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Liouvillian number (page does not exist)\">Liouvillian</a>. It can be read right after its parent article <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">rational approximation of famous numbers</a>. The proof of the non-Liouvillian character of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/3/023f0d3201106a8fb6bdfe2f539eb1726736a453.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\ln 2$\" width=\"26\" height=\"12\" /> is much easier than that for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/c/f2ca003a7da0de4994b4733e203b74ff52d42553.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\pi$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" />, but somewhat more difficult than that for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/a/a3a59bb1293ee3f6dec19de4019a7178874219ae.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$e$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" />. \n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"A_Useful_Integral\">A Useful Integral</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Rational_approximation_of_famous_numbers&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: A Useful Integral\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Everyone knows an integral representation for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/3/023f0d3201106a8fb6bdfe2f539eb1726736a453.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\ln 2$\" width=\"26\" height=\"12\" />. It comes right from the definition of the natural <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Logarithm\" title=\"Logarithm\">logarithm</a>: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/6/9e660182ad8b23c56c8b21588a9465b6ed1bd0aa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\ln 2=\\int_0^1\\frac {1}{1+x}\\,dx$\" style=\"vertical-align: -16px\" width=\"148\" height=\"43\" />. Let us look at what will happen if we replace <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> in the numerator by some polynomial <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/f/6efaaf74e576202fad965134bc49c6a34aac4c30.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"41\" height=\"20\" /> of degree <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> with integer coefficients. Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/b/d/5bd9aaacbce467ba64e7c00756304f50d34f28cb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(x)-P(-1)=(1+x)R(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"229\" height=\"18\" /> where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/8/a/d8a770d4b0f868f94b7bf062e8ccaeb173071eaa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R(x)=\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1} c_k x^k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"125\" height=\"50\" /> is some polynomial of degree <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/d/4bdca93962a2beaffc172ca394e04fe05bd74ffd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" /> with integer coefficients, we see that \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/9/f3988fbd76b1799492ea8fc2988ef2c47f5ff97a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\int_0^1\\frac{P(x)}{1+x}\\,dx=\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\\frac {c_k}{k+1}+P(-1)\\ln 2.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"302\" height=\"50\" />\n</p><p>Let now <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/a/2eacbcb991af87d495f8689ac0e952b51317dbaa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"23\" height=\"15\" /> be the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Least_common_multiple\" title=\"Least common multiple\">least common multiple</a> of the numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/2/512cb42f3c103d194a9cf8555233e472978fb37a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1,2,\\dots, n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"77\" height=\"15\" />. Then\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/b/1/cb1c874965a965baa85a9e44eb6e96bbf0d098ca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D_n\\int_0^1\\frac{P(x)}{1+x}\\,dx=P_n-Q_n\\ln 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -16px\" width=\"244\" height=\"43\" />\n</p><p>with some integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/4/c/54c51a4acedda645113145d008dbfb50d35f1da3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"23\" height=\"17\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/5/9b50676b8923aa77b765e63fd34717ccd0c7d032.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Q_n=-D_nP(-1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"135\" height=\"18\" />.\nIt remains to choose a polynomial <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/f/6efaaf74e576202fad965134bc49c6a34aac4c30.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"41\" height=\"20\" /> that makes the integral small.\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Polynomial\">Polynomial <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/f/6efaaf74e576202fad965134bc49c6a34aac4c30.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"41\" height=\"20\" /></span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Rational_approximation_of_famous_numbers&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Polynomial\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>We shall just take our \"magic polynomial\" <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/b/a1b863b58f330a84b8588daef43b0f95e533f337.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(x):=\\frac{1}{n!}\\left(\\frac{d}{dx}\\right)^n[x^n(1-x)^n]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"249\" height=\"46\" />\n(see the <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">parent article</a> for its properties).\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Estimates_of_the_Integral\">Estimates of the Integral</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Rational_approximation_of_famous_numbers&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Estimates of the Integral\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Integrating by parts <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> times, we see that\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/b/57b76d8f57eb39097698afe0674498f5e53c61ec.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\int_0^1\\frac{P(x)}{1+x}\\,dx= (-1)^n\\int_0^1\\left[\\frac{x(1-x)}{1+x}\\right]^n\\,\\frac{dx}{1+x}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"347\" height=\"44\" />.\nSince <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/d/e/0deb6864ffdf5249aa02456706b95d22a06f5dd1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\max_{0&lt;x&lt;1}x(1-x)=\\frac 14$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"146\" height=\"37\" /> and since it is attained only at the point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/3/fa3d4bda581e523faf08a04c3c11b21bce333d8e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=\\frac 12$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"46\" height=\"37\" /> where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/b/57b56877dd9209f946b9fb697326112d389c3970.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1+x&gt;1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"74\" height=\"13\" />, we can conclude that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/b/1/db1db255d3db70f74a14ababb9de7807f0a1b35a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Gamma=\\max_{0&lt;x&lt;1}\\frac{x(1-x)}{1+x}&lt;\\frac 14$\" style=\"vertical-align: -14px\" width=\"186\" height=\"40\" />. The absolute value of our integral does not exceed <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/2/6/226bbd7427245dc58d1a954247c13c3563d4fafd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Gamma^n$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"19\" height=\"13\" /> for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />. To estimate it from below, just notice that the integrand is at least <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/3/5/9350abe328a00b7603254bc8da08cd78e270f242.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac 12\\left(\\frac 2 {15}\\right)^n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"72\" height=\"44\" /> for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/e/f/aef56fd3ec0e1239d52a0ec761701033ee27361f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac 1 3 &lt; x&lt; \\frac 2 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"87\" height=\"38\" />, so the absolute value of our integral is at least <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/a/f/5af534d61b1f684aa4e790a9a5454e9297d9f98e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac 16\\left(\\frac 2 {15}\\right)^n&gt;\\left(\\frac 1 8\\right)^n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"145\" height=\"44\" /> for large <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />.\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Estimate_of\">Estimate of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/a/2eacbcb991af87d495f8689ac0e952b51317dbaa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"23\" height=\"15\" /></span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Rational_approximation_of_famous_numbers&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Estimate of\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Since the largest possible power of a given prime <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/3/003439db6c1abe32ff1728e44766ea41a1b74042.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p\\le n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"45\" height=\"15\" /> that can divide one of the numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/3/8/838228ac8ee711eda2bdae0818393f87407dbc66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1,2,\\dots,n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"77\" height=\"15\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/8/3/38308aad46c9190982150aee0f1939a8c01a8931.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left\\lfloor\\frac{\\log n}{\\log p}\\right\\rfloor$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"58\" height=\"43\" /> where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/e/2/ee2cc9f853585ef08c58f3622fecb28abfaa2e77.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\lfloor\\cdot\\rfloor$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"18\" height=\"19\" /> is the floor function, we have \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/8/9f84e0c9c59707b34a70241729f76a98c2d65466.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D_n=\\prod_{p\\mathrm{\\ prime,}\\,p\\le n}p^{\\left\\lfloor\\frac{\\log n}{\\log p}\\right\\rfloor}\\le \\left(\\prod_{p\\mathrm{\\ prime,}\\,p\\le \\sqrt n}n\\right)\\cdot\\left( \\prod_{p\\mathrm{\\ prime,}\\,\\sqrt n&lt;p\\le n}p\\right)\\le n^{\\sqrt n}\\cdot\\prod_{p\\mathrm{\\ prime,}\\,p\\le n}p\\le n^{\\sqrt n}\\cdot 4^n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -27px\" width=\"746\" height=\"64\" />\n</p><p>according to <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Prime_Number_Theorem\" title=\"Prime Number Theorem\">Chebyshev's estimate</a>. Also, we clearly have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/d/10d2d723af7f967dbae3ca3e187b186a19530933.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D_n\\ge 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"59\" height=\"17\" />. Thus the absolute value of the product <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/e/8fecd50682484700d68370f53e87447af3cf5a47.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D_n\\int_0^1\\frac{P(x)}{1+x}\\,dx$\" style=\"vertical-align: -16px\" width=\"123\" height=\"43\" />\nis between <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/3/a335bb56f8f13dfab843a7043b84193c58c173f8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1/8)^n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"52\" height=\"20\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/4/cc456132d813f3c60edddad6099995d35710fcd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(4\\Gamma)^n\\cdot n^{\\sqrt n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"87\" height=\"21\" /> for large <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />. Note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/b/1/cb1772d38605753cd5b6aebecaef0ac945f066a2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n^{\\sqrt n}$\" width=\"30\" height=\"17\" /> grows slower than any geometric progression, so the upper bound can be replaced by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/5/c/95c758c6c67152a88f315aed6a99331512aa3791.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gamma^n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"18\" height=\"16\" /> with any <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/6/f/66f77d773e8e6943188ff8075f318d0360f472ea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gamma\\in(4\\Gamma, 1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"82\" height=\"18\" />. In order to apply the main theorem from the <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">parent article</a>, it remains to show that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/9/d29e04bbd77de0b9795acf7763c8fa9a8182806c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Q_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"22\" height=\"16\" /> do not grow too fast.\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Estimate_of_2\">Estimate of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/9/d29e04bbd77de0b9795acf7763c8fa9a8182806c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Q_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"22\" height=\"16\" /></span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Rational_approximation_of_famous_numbers&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: Estimate of\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>We already saw that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/a/2eacbcb991af87d495f8689ac0e952b51317dbaa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"23\" height=\"15\" /> grows not much faster than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/7/6c78734258d2d82aeb28c1631e8f1798154c888d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4^n$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"17\" height=\"13\" />. As to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/e/a/0eadb50cf56185ae8434a937d4008d6fc80f1be2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|P(-1)|$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"59\" height=\"18\" />, it does not exceed the sum of the absolute values of the coefficients of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/f/6efaaf74e576202fad965134bc49c6a34aac4c30.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"41\" height=\"20\" />, which is not greater than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/d/f/4df6714777a7971d5bfac87a800af2afc4933d62.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8^n$\" width=\"17\" height=\"12\" />. Thus <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/6/9/16934ea86692d09c8a971e31e92cecc22e54444f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|Q_n|$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"31\" height=\"18\" /> grow not much faster than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/0/2/202fc77b2e99b6d103c04252d6ec2727b834a880.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$32^n$\" width=\"26\" height=\"12\" />, and we are done.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Rational_approximation_of_famous_numbers&amp;action=edit&amp;section=10\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Euler%27s_constant\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Euler&#39;s constant\">Euler's constant</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Pi\" title=\"Pi\">Pi</a></li></ul>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018090928\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.050 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.066 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 535/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 0/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 3/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 19938/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    0.000      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:2142:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018090928 and revision id 22820. Rendering was triggered because: page-view\n -->\n</div>"}